00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:57.170
It's great.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:00.030
I get to hear tech,
tech, tech all day long.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:03.080
But when I go home
I watch tech videos,

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:05.470
but it's just like there's
too much tech jargon in them.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:06.500
Am I right?

00:09:06.500 --> 00:09:08.200
Like, I want to
watch the tech video.

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:09.980
I want to get all
the techie goodness,

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.650
but I don't want to
hear about the tech.

00:09:11.650 --> 00:09:14.230
I want the tech video
without the tech.

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:16.660
Yeah, everyone has
this sort of problem?

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:19.750
Yeah, I want tech
videos without the tech,

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.930
and so luckily I've
managed to glue together

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.670
some tools to achieve this.

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:27.787
Now I don't know if you've
been on the floor yet,

00:09:27.787 --> 00:09:29.620
but if you've been over
near the AI section,

00:09:29.620 --> 00:09:32.260
we actually have a demo that
shows our Video Intelligence

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.177
API.

00:09:33.177 --> 00:09:35.010
Who here has used the
Video Intelligence API

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:36.010
or heard of it?

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:37.090
A couple of people, yeah.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.160
So we had this demo,
and it shows us some

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:41.830
of the capabilities of the API.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:44.050
And one of them is
object tracking.

00:09:44.050 --> 00:09:47.300
So it'll do, sort of, a
base object detection,

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:48.550
and it'll do all that for you.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:50.560
You don't need to
train the model.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.710
But it also does this cool
thing, which is transcription.

00:09:53.710 --> 00:09:56.110
So if you've ever used
the text to speech,

00:09:56.110 --> 00:09:58.650
it's basically the exact
same API that runs alongside.

00:09:58.650 --> 00:10:00.400
But it just saves you
having to scrape out

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:02.167
the audio from your video file.

00:10:02.167 --> 00:10:04.750
So you give it-- you give it the
video, tell it transcribe it,

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:05.968
and it will do it.

00:10:05.968 --> 00:10:08.260
The cool thing about it is
that when it transcribes it,

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:10.930
not only does it tell you
the whole block of text

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:14.980
that is in the video, but
it will tell you per word

00:10:14.980 --> 00:10:18.912
when each word was said
at a specific second time.

00:10:18.912 --> 00:10:19.870
So this is interesting.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:23.890
I could potentially
use this to maybe build

00:10:23.890 --> 00:10:27.070
an app that will find the words
that I didn't want to hear

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:29.410
and maybe beep them out for me.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:30.070
So that's cool.

00:10:30.070 --> 00:10:32.390
I could potentially do that.

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:35.050
But hearing the
words is one thing.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.940
I mean, seeing the
person move their lips

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.550
to the word, that's just
as bad as hearing the word.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:42.640
Because speaking in conferences,
you learn a bit of lip reading.

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:46.480
So is there some other tool
that we can use to solve this?

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.649
So we had the Vision API.

00:10:48.649 --> 00:10:50.191
And what that does
if you've used it,

00:10:50.191 --> 00:10:55.330
if you upload an image of a face
and tell it you're not a robot,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:58.825
it will detect the individual
points of your face for you.

00:10:58.825 --> 00:11:02.390
And it'll do this with
pretty good accuracy.

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.370
So then the task is simple.

00:11:04.370 --> 00:11:07.890
We take the Video Intelligence
API, transcribe it,

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:11.320
detect the words we don't want
to hear, run the Vision API,

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.150
find the position of the mouth,
and then with that information

00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:18.130
we can beep out the audio
and beep out the mouth.

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:21.560
So I've recruited
my friend, Greg--

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.330
or not Greg.

00:11:22.330 --> 00:11:25.420
Oh, that's terrible, Gabe.

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:27.310
Gosh that's recorded.

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:29.480
So Gabe is a good
friend of mine.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.630
I've known him years.

00:11:31.630 --> 00:11:32.200
This is him.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:33.970
I've taken this video today.

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:36.430
He built this really
cool edge demo.

00:11:36.430 --> 00:11:37.920
And I got him to say--

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.640
so Gabe, what have you built?

00:11:39.640 --> 00:11:42.430
GABE: We've built a demo that's
doing vision intelligence

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:45.880
with a model that we've
built in AutoML on GCP,

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:49.240
and it compiles down
to OCR classification

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.010
to run on our demo.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.200
It compiles it down
to the edge TPU

00:11:53.200 --> 00:11:56.020
so that we can run it locally.

