interested in system

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:20.260
monitoring, basic
sysadmin stuff.

00:15:20.260 --> 00:15:21.610
So we have Stackdriver.

00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:23.630
Stackdriver's pretty cool.

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:25.450
But a problem that
I have with it

00:15:25.450 --> 00:15:29.090
is that you only get
data once a minute.

00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:32.240
So it's a 60 second
interval, which is fine most

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:34.490
of the time for historical
graphs and stuff like that.

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:37.032
But sometimes you want to know
what's happening in real time.

00:15:37.032 --> 00:15:40.120
Real time like--
real time metrics

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:45.190
are very important for
debugging in a hurry and also

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:49.150
like our brains are very
good at pattern recognition.

00:15:49.150 --> 00:15:53.230
And so if we see
graphs all the time

00:15:53.230 --> 00:15:55.180
when there's an
anomaly on the graph,

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:56.680
your brain is going
to recognize it.

00:15:56.680 --> 00:15:57.910
Because your brain
knows something's

00:15:57.910 --> 00:15:59.910
not right here, because
it's used to the pattern

00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.460
that it's used to seeing.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.210
And so you can do
that with this.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.960
But if you have those
graphs in real time,

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.418
you get that feedback
much quicker.

00:16:06.418 --> 00:16:08.127
And so I've always
been interested in how

00:16:08.127 --> 00:16:10.930
do you get information out of
these things and into something

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:13.340
that you can actually
see in the real world.

